Description: Enable non-root mode by default for Android (uid is u0_aXXX, never 0).
Origin: haisa-des
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-07-30

Android app processes run as non-root uid (u0_aXXX). dpkg has a FORCE_NON_ROOT
flag (enabled via --force-not-root) that:
  1. Makes modstatdb_open() use msdbrw_write instead of msdbrw_needsuperuser
  2. Makes forcible_nonroot_error() ignore EPERM from chown/fchmod
  3. Makes can_invoke_hooks() return true without uid==0

But apt calls dpkg internally without --force-not-root, so the flag is never
set. The cleanest fix is to flip FORCE_NON_ROOT to FORCETYPE_ENABLED by default
(on Android there is no real root, so this is always the desired mode).

This single patch covers:
  - "requested operation requires superuser privilege" (dbmodify.c)
  - "error setting ownership/permissions" (archives.c chown/fchmod EPERM)

--- a/src/common/force.c
+++ b/src/common/force.c
@@ -94,7 +94,7 @@
 	}, {
 		"not-root",
 		FORCE_NON_ROOT,
-		FORCETYPE_DISABLED,
+		FORCETYPE_ENABLED,
 		N_("Try to (de)install things even when not root"),
 	}, {
 		"bad-path",
